## Generate candidates, reject violations

### What you will learn

Separate choice rules from integrity constraints.

### Choose exactly one option

A choice rule creates alternatives; the lower and upper bound both equal one. The integrity constraint rejects the unavailable person. Two complete assignments remain.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    person(ada;ben;cy).
    1 { assigned(Person) : person(Person) } 1.
    :- assigned(ada).
    #show assigned/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

true = result.exhausted
[["assigned(ben)"], ["assigned(cy)"]] = Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))
Course.display(result)
```

### Disjoin heads, not body conditions

In a rule body, commas mean that all conditions must hold. A semicolon in a disjunctive head permits alternatives. With no other support, the following positive disjunction has two minimal stable models. Use separate rules when either of two bodies should derive the same head.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    lamp; lantern.
    light :- lamp.
    light :- lantern.
    #show lamp/0.
    #show lantern/0.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[["lamp"], ["lantern"]] = Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))
Course.display(result)
```

### Try it yourself

Allow at most one assigned person by changing the lower bound from 1 to 0. How many assignments survive the constraint?

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

There are three: assign Ben, assign Cy, or assign nobody. The upper bound still prevents selecting both.

</details>

## Make atoms optional

A choice head permits its atoms to be selected. Other rules can still require consequences or eliminate choices. Enumerate all models to see the allowed combinations.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  {a} :- b.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  b :- a.
  c :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  b :- a.
  b :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  { b }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- c.
  { b } :- d.
  c.
  d :- c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  { b } :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  { b } :- c.
  c :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  { b } :- a.
  { c } :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  { b } :- d.
  { c } :- not d.
  d :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :- not a, not b.
  d :- a, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :- not a, b.
  d :- a, not b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  b :- a.
  a :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  b :- a.
  a :- c.
  c :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  b :- a.
  a :- b.
  { c } :- not a, not b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  b :- a.
  a :- b.
  { c } :- a, b.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Combine choices with positive recursion

Separate the optional choice from the consequences it supports. Positive recursion propagates support but does not independently justify unsupported atoms.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  { a } :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- a, b.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b, c.
  { b } :- a, c.
  c :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  b :- c.
  c :- not d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  b.
  c :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b, d.
  { b } :- a.
  b :- d.
  c :- b, not a.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  { b }.
  { c } :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  { a }.
  { c } :- not b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- c.
  { c } :- a.
  { a }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b, c.
  { b } :- c.
  { c } :- a.
  { c }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  { c } :- d.
  { d } :- c.
  { c }.
  b :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- a.
  { c } :- d.
  { d } :- c.
  c.
  { b } :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  { c } :- d.
  { d } :- c.
  { c }.
  b :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  { c } :- d.
  { d } :- c.
  c.
  { b } :- d.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Filter candidate models

A headless rule rejects a candidate whenever all its body conditions hold. Compare the same program before and after each constraint, and identify the eliminated candidates.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  b :- a.
  :- not b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  b :- not a.
  :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  b :- a.
  c :- not a.
  :- a, b.
  :- not a, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a }.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a.
  :- not a, not b.
  :- a, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :- a, not b.
  d :- not a, b.
  :- c.
  :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- a, not b.
  :- not a, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b.
  { b } :- c.
  { c } :- a.
  { a }.
  :- a, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- b, c.
  { b } :- c.
  { c } :- a.
  { c }.
  :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :- a, not b.
  :- not a, c.
  :- b, not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :- not a, b.
  :- a, not c.
  :- not b, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  :- a, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- not a, b.
  :- not b, a.
  :- a, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a } :- not b.
  :- not a, not b.
  { b } :- not c.
  :- not b, not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  :- not a.
  a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  :-.
  """,
  ~S"""
  :-.
  a.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Bound a choice

The bounds around a choice restrict the number of selected literals. Enumerate the models and check those counts directly, including the lower and upper boundaries.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :-   { a; b } 0.
  d :- 1 { a; b }.
  e :- 1 { a; b } 1.
  f :- 2 { a; b }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
    :-   { a; b } 0.
  d :- 1 { a; b }.
  e :- 1 { a; b } 1.
    :- 2 { a; b }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :-   { not a; not b } 0.
  d :- 1 { not a; not b }.
  e :- 1 { not a; not b } 1.
  f :- 2 { not a; not b }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :-   { a; not b } 0.
  d :- 1 { a; not b }.
  e :- 1 { a; not b } 1.
  f :- 2 { a; not b }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :-   { a; b; c } 0.
  :- 2 { a; b; c } 2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :- 3 { a; b; c }.
  :- 1 { a; b; c } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :- not 1 { a; b; c } 2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :- not { a; b; c } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :- 1 { a; b } 1.
  :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :- not 1 { a; b } 1.
  :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  { c } :- 1 { a; b } 1.
  :- not 2 { a; b; c } 2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a ; b }.
  { c } :- { a; b } 0.
  :- not 1 { a; b; c } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- not 1 { a; b } 1.
  b :- a.
  a :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- not 1 { a; b } 1.
  :- 1 { a; b } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  c :- 1 { a }, { b } 0.
  d :- 2 { not a; b }.
  :- 1 { c }.
  :- 1 { d }.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Combine bounds and conditions

Read each choice bound together with the rule body that activates it. Compare programs that look similar but constrain different sets or activate under different conditions.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- not 1 { a; b } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b; c }.
  :- not 2 { a; b; c }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  2 { a; b; c }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- not 1 { a; b } 1.
  { b; c }.
  :- not 1 { b; c } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b } 1.
  1 { b; c } 1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b }.
  :- not { a; b } 1.
  { b; c }.
  :- not 1 { b; c }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b } 1.
  1 { b; c }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b } 1.
  c :- a.
  d :- b.
  :- { c; d } 0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b } 1.
  c :- a.
  d :- b.
  :- 1 { c ; d }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b }.
  1 { c; d } 1 :- a.
  :- b, 2 { a; c; d }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b }.
  1 { c; d } 1 :- a.
  :- not 2 { a; b; c; d }.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a; b } 1.
  1 { b; c } :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a; b } 1.
  1 { b; c } 1 :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a } 1 :- { b } 0.
  1 { b } 1 :- { c } 0.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Generate and filter structured choices

Use the domain to generate optional atoms, then apply constraints to rule out combinations. Check that every remaining shown set satisfies both the choice bounds and the restrictions.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  { a(Item) : b(Item) }.
  b(1). b(2).
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a(Item) : b(Item) }.
  b(1). b(2). b(3).
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item) : b(Item) } 1.
  b(1). b(2).
  c(Item) :- a(Item).
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item) : b(Item) } 1.
  b(1). b(2). b(3).
  c(Item) :- a(Item).
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item,Other) : b(Item), c(Other) } 1.
  b(1). b(2). c(3). c(4).
  d(Item,Other) :- a(Item,Other).
  #show d/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a(Item,Other) : b(Item), c(Other) } 1.
  b(1). b(2). c(3). c(4).
  d(Item,Other) :- a(Item,Other).
  #show d/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a(Item) : b(Item) }.
  b(1). b(2).
  :- b(Item), Other=Item-1, a(Other).
  """,
  ~S"""
  { a(Item) : b(Item) }.
  b(1). b(2). b(3).
  :- b(Item), Other=Item-1, a(Other).
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item) : b(Item) } 1.
  b(1). b(2).
  c(Item) :- a(Item).
  :- b(Item), not a(Item).
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item) : b(Item) } 1.
  b(1).
  c(Item) :- a(Item).
  :- b(Item), not a(Item).
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item,Other) : b(Item) } 1 :- c(Other).
  b(1). b(2). c(3). c(4).
  d(Item,Other) :- a(Item,Other).
  #show d/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  1 { a(Item,Other) : b(Item) } 1 :- c(Other).
  b(1). b(2). b(3). c(4).
  d(Item,Other) :- a(Item,Other).
  #show d/2.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Choices, consequences and constraints

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace()
```

The examples separate three modeling operations: choose candidate atoms, derive consequences, and reject candidates that violate a condition. File cells write to a disposable workspace; subsequent solves load those files through the NIF.

A rule with a normal head derives that atom when its body holds. A headless rule is an integrity constraint: it excludes models where its body holds. A choice rule makes alternatives available without requiring every alternative to be true. Read the displayed models as sets, regardless of their enumeration order.

### Follow the small examples

Run each file-writing cell before its solve. Compare the models after adding a rule or changing the domain.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example1.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
b :- a.
:- not b.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example1.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
{a}.
b :- a.
:- not b.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example2.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
 b :- a.
{c}:- not a.
 d :- not b, not c.
   :- c, not a.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example2.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
{a}.
{b}.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
{b}.
{a}.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Use variables over a domain

A variable stands for ground terms supplied by the program. Adding domain facts can create additional choices, while a constraint can eliminate choices that were previously allowed.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example3.lp", ~S"""
 n(1).
{a(Item)}:- n(Item).
 b(Item) :- a(Item).
      :- n(Item), not b(Item).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example3.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
n(1,2).
{a(Item,Other)}:- n(Item,Other).
 b(Item)   :- a(Item,Other).
        :- n(Item,Other), not b(Item).
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example4.lp", ~S"""
 n(1).   n(2).
{a(Item)}:- n(Item).
 b(Item) :- a(Item).
      :- n(Item), not b(Item).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example4.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example5.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
b :- c.
c :- a.
a :- b.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example5.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example6.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
{b} :- c.
{c} :- a.
{a} :- b.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example6.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "traveling.lp", ~S"""
start(a).
road(a,b). road(b,c). road(c,d). road(d,a).
visit(Other) :- road(Item,Other), start(Item).
visit(Other) :- road(Item,Other), visit(Item).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "traveling.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "numbers.lp", ~S"""
next(1,2). next(2,3). next(3,4). next(4,5).
odd(1).
even(N) :-  odd(M), next(M,N).
 odd(N) :- even(M), next(M,N).
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "numbers.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "negative_recursion.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
b :- not c.
c :- not a.
a :- not b.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "negative_recursion.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "positive_recursion.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
b :- c.
c :- a.
a :- b.
d :- e, not a.
e :- d, not b.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "positive_recursion.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example7.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
 b  :- c.
{c} :- a.
{a} :- b.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example7.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example8.lp", ~S"""
{a}.
{b} :- a.
 c  :- b.
 d  :- c.
{e} :- d.
 f  :- e.
 g  :- f.
 a  :- g.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example8.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "flying.lp", ~S"""
 start(a). flight(a,b). road(b,c).
road(c,d). flight(d,e). road(e,a).
  visit(Item)   :- start(Item).
  visit(Other)   :- road(Item,Other), visit(Item).
{ fly(Item,Other) } :- flight(Item,Other), visit(Item).
  visit(Other)   :- fly(Item,Other).
#show visit/1.
#show fly/2.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "flying.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

